::meta{crate: tomet-workspace, dependencies: list(tomet-ast, tomet-config, tomet-expr, tomet-formatter, tomet-indexer, tomet-load-config, tomet-parser, tomet-printer, tomet-search, tomet-semantics, tomet-transform, tomet-tree), dependents: list(tomet-cli, tomet-load, tomet-tests)}

=[tomet-workspace]

Workspace-level batch refactoring, structural search, metadata editing, and I/O execution.

Provides:

- (diff) `FileDiff` model and disk persistence.
- (refactor) Refactoring pipeline across `.tmt` files.
- (structural) Structural search & replace engine across a workspace.
- (batch_meta) Batch metadata update engine.
- (blueprint) Blueprint discovery and document instantiation.

==[Module batch_meta]

Batch metadata editing across workspace files.

===[Structs]

====[struct MetaFile]

Represents a workspace file with its metadata.

====[struct BatchMetaEngine]

==[Module blueprint]

Blueprint discovery and file instantiation across a workspace.

A vault declares its blueprints in its config, as a list of paths, and
each blueprint names itself with `::blueprint(X)`. Nothing is derived
from a filename and nothing is searched for by convention.

That is a change from what this did, and the reason is in this
feature's own history. Lookup used to try `templates/`,
`.tomet/templates/` and `docs/examples/blueprints/`, times three
filename spellings -- which is how `docs/docs.settings.tmt` came to
point at a file that does not exist and still resolve: the misses
were invisible and one hit. Folding that to a single convention made
the surface smaller but kept the silence. A declared list moves the
error to the declaration: a path that is not there is reported once,
where it is written, instead of at every document that wanted it.

A name -> path map was rejected as a duplicate: the name would appear
in the key, in the path, and again in the file's own `::blueprint(X)`.

===[Structs]

====[struct Unfilled]

An expression in a blueprint that had no value, and so stands in the new
document as written.

====[struct Instantiation]

A new document made from a blueprint, not yet written anywhere.

====[struct Created]

A new document written to disk by [`create_file_from_blueprint`].

===[Functions]

====[fn list_blueprints]

Every blueprint `config` declares, as `(name, path)`, sorted by name.

A declared path that is missing or does not name itself is skipped
here and reported by [`declaration_errors`]; listing and creating
should not fail wholesale because one entry rotted.

====[fn declaration_errors]

What is wrong with the declared list: paths that do not exist, do not
parse, or do not name themselves, and names claimed twice.

Reported at the declaration, which is the whole point of declaring.

====[fn find_blueprint]

The blueprint named `name`, among the ones `config` declares.

====[fn instantiate_blueprint_file]

Instantiates a blueprint file into formatted Tomet source text for the new
document `target`, a path relative to the vault.

`vars` are the values passed to `tomet new --var key=value`, read in the
blueprint as `${var.key}`. A name nothing declares is refused, and so is a
variable that has neither a value nor a `default`: the blueprint's `::vars`
(over the vault's) says which there are. The document being created is `self`, so `${self.filename}` is the new file's
name. `${generate.now()}` is written in the offset the settings give
`created` (or `modified`), UTC if neither has one.

An expression that has no value stays in the new document as written, so
the person who made it can see what was left to fill in; each one is also
returned in [`Instantiation::unfilled`], so the caller can say so.

====[fn create_file_from_blueprint]

Creates a new document from a blueprint and writes it to disk.

What the document was left to fill in is in [`Created::unfilled`]; it is
written anyway, since the expressions are still there to be filled in by
hand.

==[Module diff]

File difference model and safe disk saving operations.

===[Structs]

====[struct FileDiff]

Represents the modification state and diff of a file.

===[Functions]

====[fn save_file_diffs]

Saves all modified `FileDiff`s to disk, returning the count of saved files.

==[Module refactor]

Workspace and document refactoring pipeline.

===[Structs]

====[struct RefactorOptions]

Configuration options for workspace and document refactoring passes.

====[struct RefactorReport]

Discovers and refactors all `.tmt` files in `dir_or_file`.
What a sweep found: the per-file diffs, and the files it could not
look at.

The errors are carried rather than dropped. One unreadable or
unparseable file should not abort the sweep -- that is
`check_vault`'s convention too -- but it must not vanish either.
`--check` is a guard, and a guard that stays quiet about the files it
skipped has its hole exactly where it matters most: a file broken
enough not to parse is the likeliest one to still carry the spelling
being migrated away from.

===[Functions]

====[fn refactor_document]

Applies configured refactoring transformations to a parsed [`Document`].

====[fn refactor_source]

Refactors source text, returning the transformed and formatted text alongside the changes count.

@strong[Lossy: this drops comments.] It parses to a `Document` and prints
the whole document back, and `tomet_ast` has no comment node at all --
`//` and `/* */` exist in the source text and nowhere in the tree, so
anything that round-trips through the AST loses them. Measured, not
assumed: a file carrying `::meta{ type: note }` and a `//` line comes
back with the comment gone.

The counting half is sound, because it counts rule hits rather than a
text diff -- `tomet refactor --check` is safe to run on anything, and
`FileDiff::is_changed` is `changes_count > 0` precisely so a file no
rule touched is never rewritten. It is `-i` on a file a rule @em[does]
touch that costs you the comments.

====[fn refactor_workspace]

